### Tone: Rule slide: how to answer. Everyone gets the same question and works it out in the answer block, one step per
### line; the last line is the answer, and everyone sees the working at the reveal. Clinical instructions, one jab.

rules-sheet-1 = Everyone gets the same question. Nobody knows the answer. Work it out one step per line. The last line is your answer.
rules-sheet-2 = Break the problem into small steps, one per line. Your working will be shown to everyone afterwards. Plan accordingly.
rules-sheet-3 = You may use variables and comments. The last line is your answer. Everyone will see the rest. For science.
rules-sheet-4 = Nobody here knows the answer. That’s the point. Estimate it, step by step, and make your working look deliberate.
rules-sheet-5 = Write one calculation per line. Name things if it helps. Your last line is your answer, so make it a number, not a cry for help.
rules-sheet-6 = Guessing is allowed. Reasoning is encouraged. Both will be displayed to the group afterwards, without mercy.
rules-sheet-7 = Same question for everyone. Different levels of preparation, clearly. Show your steps; I enjoy reading them aloud in my head.
rules-sheet-8 = Use the answer block like a lab notebook. One step per line. The final line is what I grade. The rest is what I mock.
rules-sheet-9 = You won’t know the answer. Nobody does. Break it into things you *do* know, multiply, and hope.
rules-sheet-10 = Lock in when you’re done. Or don’t: when time runs out, your last line counts anyway. I find that very efficient.
